---
import Tool from '../../components/Tool.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const example = `a lighthouse on a cliff at dusk, oil painting, <lora:my-style-v2:0.8>
Negative prompt: blurry, low quality, watermark
Steps: 30, Sampler: DPM++ 2M, Schedule type: Karras, CFG scale: 6.5, Seed: 1234567890, Size: 832x1216, Model hash: 0a1b2c3d4e, Model: myPrivateMix_v3, Lora hashes: "my-style-v2: 5f6e7d8c9b0a", Version: v1.10.1`;
---
<Base
  lang="en"
  heading="Remove Stable Diffusion metadata from your images"
  title="Remove Stable Diffusion Metadata (PNG Info) — PromptWipe"
  description="Remove the prompt, seed, model and LoRA names that AUTOMATIC1111, Forge, Fooocus and InvokeAI save in PNG, JPEG and WebP. In your browser: nothing is uploaded."
>
  <p class="lead">
    AUTOMATIC1111, Forge, Fooocus and InvokeAI can write your prompt, negative prompt, seed, sampler, model and LoRA
    names into the images they save. Drop a PNG, JPEG or WebP below: PromptWipe shows that text, then removes it
    without re-encoding the picture. Nothing is uploaded, and invisible watermarks in the pixels stay.
  </p>
  <Tool lang="en" />
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Does Stable Diffusion save the prompt in the image?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes, by default. AUTOMATIC1111 (A1111) writes a block of text, which it calls the infotext, into every image it
      saves: the prompt, the negative prompt and every setting needed to make the same picture again. Forge, which is
      built on the A1111 code, uses the same format.
    </p>
    <p>Where the text goes depends on the file format:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>PNG</strong>, the default: a text chunk named <code>parameters</code>. Text that extensions attach to
        the image is saved alongside it, under its own key.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>JPEG and WebP</strong>: the EXIF <code>UserComment</code> tag, stored as UTF-16 text (the “unicode”
        encoding of the piexif library).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AVIF</strong> gets the same EXIF tag and <strong>GIF</strong> a GIF comment. PromptWipe does not open
        AVIF or GIF files yet: it says so and gives you a link to request the format.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Anyone who gets the file can read this text. A1111’s own PNG Info tab shows it and can send the settings back to
      txt2img, and the <a href="/view-ai-image-prompt/">PromptWipe viewer</a> shows it in your browser.
    </p>

    <h2>What does the <code>parameters</code> text contain?</h2>
    <p>
      Three parts: the prompt, a line that starts with “Negative prompt:”, and one long line of settings. Here is an
      invented example in the A1111 format:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>The prompt, including any <code>&lt;lora:…&gt;</code> tags you typed.</li>
      <li>The negative prompt.</li>
      <li>The steps, sampler, CFG scale, seed and size: enough to make the same image again with the same model.</li>
      <li>The hash and file name of the checkpoint, and a hash for each LoRA or embedding used.</li>
      <li>The web UI version, and the values of extensions, which can add their own entries to this line.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why remove it before sharing?</h2>
    <p>
      Because the file says more than the picture. A prompt can be hours of trial and error that you would rather keep
      for yourself. The seed and settings let anyone copy your image exactly. Model and LoRA names, with their hashes,
      can point to a private or unreleased model, or to a style you trained for a client.
    </p>
    <p>
      The file name can give things away as well. With A1111’s default settings, an image is saved as a number followed
      by its seed, such as <code>00012-1234567890.png</code>, and with some settings the start of the prompt is added.
      PromptWipe keeps your file name and adds <code>-clean</code>, so rename the copy if the name says too much.
    </p>

    <h2>What do Fooocus and InvokeAI write?</h2>
    <h3>Fooocus</h3>
    <p>
      Fooocus writes metadata only when “Save Metadata to Images” is ticked (Advanced › Developer Debug Mode › Debug
      Tools; off by default). A PNG then gets two text chunks, <code>parameters</code> and <code>fooocus_scheme</code>.
      A JPEG or WebP gets three EXIF tags: <code>UserComment</code> with the parameters, <code>Software</code> with
      “Fooocus v” and the version, and <code>MakerNote</code> with the scheme. With the “a1111” scheme the text uses
      the A1111 format above; with the “fooocus” scheme it is JSON.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whatever that option says, Fooocus also keeps a <code>log.html</code> page in its outputs folder that lists each
      image with its prompt and settings. It stays on your computer, but do not share that folder as it is.
    </p>
    <h3>InvokeAI</h3>
    <p>
      InvokeAI saves PNG files with up to three text chunks: <code>invokeai_metadata</code>, the generation record
      (prompts, model, seed and settings); <code>invokeai_graph</code>, the graph that ran; and
      <code>invokeai_workflow</code>, when you used a workflow. Older versions used other keys, such as
      <code>sd-metadata</code>. PromptWipe names all of them in its report.
    </p>

    <h2>How does PromptWipe remove it?</h2>
    <p>
      It rebuilds the file from its own blocks and leaves the metadata blocks out. The image data is copied byte for
      byte: nothing is re-encoded, so the quality does not change.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>PNG</strong>: every text chunk goes, whatever its type: plain <code>tEXt</code>, compressed
        <code>zTXt</code> or international <code>iTXt</code>. Some tools that re-save an image store the same parameters
        in one of the last two, which is why all three are removed. <code>eXIf</code>, <code>tIME</code> and Content
        Credentials (<code>caBX</code>) go too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>JPEG and WebP</strong>: the whole EXIF block goes, with XMP and Content Credentials. If the image had a
        rotation, only that rotation is written back.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Kept</strong>: the colour profile, so colours do not shift, and transparency or animation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Before offering the download, PromptWipe reads the new file again to check that no metadata is left, then decodes
      it in your browser and checks that its dimensions match the original’s. The report lists what was found, what was removed
      and what was kept. <a href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a> gives the details.
    </p>

    <h2>Can I stop the metadata from being written in the first place?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes, but you then lose that record for yourself. In A1111, open Settings, section Infotext, and untick “Write
      infotext to metadata of the generated image”. Leave “Create a text file with infotext next to every generated
      image” unticked too, or the same text lands in a <code>.txt</code> file next to each image. In Forge, look for the
      same options; in Fooocus, leave “Save Metadata to Images” off.
    </p>
    <p>
      A simpler habit: keep your originals with their metadata, so you can reuse a seed later, and share only cleaned
      copies. For ComfyUI files, see <a href="/remove-comfyui-workflow-from-image/">how to remove the ComfyUI
      workflow</a>. The <a href="/guides/what-metadata-do-ai-images-contain/">guide to AI image metadata</a> compares what
      each tool writes.
    </p>

    <h2>Does this hide that the image was made with AI?</h2>
    <p>
      No. It removes the text about how you made the image, not what the image is. Some Stable Diffusion code adds an
      invisible watermark to the pixels: Stability AI’s SDXL reference scripts do, and so does the diffusers SDXL
      pipeline when the invisible-watermark package is installed. PromptWipe never changes pixels, so such a watermark
      stays. If a platform or the law asks you to label AI images, label them.
    </p>

    <p class="sources">
      Sources (read in the code, November 2026): AUTOMATIC1111
      <a href="https://github.com/AUTOMATIC1111/stable-diffusion-webui/blob/master/modules/images.py">modules/images.py</a>
      (saving and file names),
      <a href="https://github.com/AUTOMATIC1111/stable-diffusion-webui/blob/master/modules/shared_options.py">modules/shared_options.py</a>
      (settings),
      <a href="https://github.com/AUTOMATIC1111/stable-diffusion-webui/blob/master/modules/infotext_utils.py">modules/infotext_utils.py</a>
      (format); Fooocus
      <a href="https://github.com/lllyasviel/Fooocus/blob/main/modules/private_logger.py">modules/private_logger.py</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/lllyasviel/Fooocus/blob/main/modules/meta_parser.py">modules/meta_parser.py</a>; InvokeAI
      <a href="https://invoke-ai.github.io/InvokeAI-7/development/architecture/media-metadata/">media metadata</a>;
      Stability AI
      <a href="https://github.com/Stability-AI/generative-models/blob/main/scripts/demo/streamlit_helpers.py">streamlit_helpers.py</a>;
      diffusers
      <a href="https://github.com/huggingface/diffusers/blob/main/src/diffusers/pipelines/stable_diffusion_xl/pipeline_stable_diffusion_xl.py">SDXL pipeline</a>;
      W3C <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/png-3/">PNG specification</a> (text chunks).
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
